# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aristotle-politics-1.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1 has "same_rulers" and "same_rulers → sedition" and "sedition → hazardous" and "gold_fixed → same_rulers" and "¬military_happy"; aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1.2 and aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1.3 have "same_rulers" and "same_rulers → sedition" and "sedition → hazardous" and "gold_fixed → same_rulers" and "¬military_happy" and "city_happy → military_happy ∨ artisans_happy ∨ multitude_happy"; they differ when only "Socrates would have persons of the same rank always in office", "this arrangement becomes the cause of sedition", "it is very hazardous to settle the magistracy as Socrates has done", "the golden particle God mixed into the soul does not pass from one to another but always stays with the same" and "the whole city is happy" are true, which the first allows
aristotle-politics-1.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1.2 and aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "Socrates would have persons of the same rank always in office", "this arrangement becomes the cause of sedition", "it is very hazardous to settle the magistracy as Socrates has done", "the golden particle God mixed into the soul does not pass from one to another but always stays with the same" and "the whole city is happy" are true and "the military are happy", "the artisans are happy" and "the multitude employed in inferior offices are happy" are false. Is S1, "It is also very hazardous to settle the magistracy as Socrates has done; for he would have persons of the same rank always in office, which becomes the cause of sedition even amongst those who are of no account, but more particularly amongst those who are of a courageous and warlike disposition; it is indeed evidently necessary that he should frame his community in this manner; for that golden particle which God has mixed up in the soul of man flies not from one to the other, but always continues with the same; for he says, that some of our species have gold, and others silver, blended in their composition from the moment of their birth: but those who are to be husbandmen and artists, brass and iron; besides, though he deprives the military of happiness, he says, that the legislator ought to make all the citizens happy; but it is impossible that the whole city can be happy, without all, or the greater, or some part of it be happy.", true then?
aristotle-politics-1.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1 has "city_happy → military_happy ∨ artisans_happy ∨ multitude_happy" and "¬artisans_happy" and "¬multitude_happy"; aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1.2 and aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1.3 have "¬artisans_happy ∧ ¬multitude_happy"; they differ when only "the whole city is happy" is true, which the second allows
aristotle-politics-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1, to: Suppose "the whole city is happy" is true and "the military are happy", "the artisans are happy" and "the multitude employed in inferior offices are happy" are false. Is S2, "For happiness is not like that numerical equality which arises from certain numbers when added together, although neither of them may separately contain it; for happiness cannot be thus added together, but must exist in every individual, as some properties belong to every integral; and if the military are not happy, who else are so? for the artisans are not, nor the multitude of those who are employed in inferior offices.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
aristotle-politics-1.md:1: argue/negation: aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1 P8 (S2): the sentence says "neither", but P8 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if the whole city is happy, then (either either the military are happy or the artisans are happy or the multitude employed in inferior offices are happy)" [in 1 of 2 runs]
aristotle-politics-1.md:1: argue/unfaithful: aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1 C (S3): the sentence has no word of "the whole city is happy": is it the sentence C came from? [in 1 of 2 runs]
aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1: argue/answers-split: no reading agrees with all 2 answers (aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1 1, aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1.2 1, aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1.3 1 of them): each is right somewhere, and the reading the answers want takes statements from more than one [in 1 of 2 runs]
